title: A redirect must not carry the bearer token to another host
updated: 2026-10-20T09:29:29Z
---

The user agent follows redirects and sends the bearer token along.

lib/WWW/PayPal/Role/HTTP.pm:56-59 builds the LWP::UserAgent with timeout => 30 only, without max_redirect => 0. The default of LWP is 7 redirects, and requests_redirectable covers GET and HEAD.

So a GET that answers 301 or 302 (a maintenance page of PayPal, a CDN, a proxy or a man in the middle) is followed, and the Authorization: Bearer <token> header goes to the redirect target. On the LWP of CPAN the header is not removed (the copy installed in /home/getty/perl5 carries a local patch for that, which cpanfile:5 does not ask for), so the foreign target holds the token, and the answer of the foreign target counts as the success of the call.

The house pattern of the fleet sets max_redirect => 0: p5-www-keycloak/lib/WWW/Keycloak.pm:127, p5-www-mikrotik/lib/WWW/Mikrotik.pm:225, p5-www-authentik:145, p5-www-idrac:271.

Decide: the user agent gets max_redirect => 0, and every 3xx is raised as an error with status, method and path.

2026-10-20 09:17 Done: the user agent is built with max_redirect => 0, so LWP follows no redirect and the Authorization: Bearer header never reaches a foreign target. A 3xx now arrives as the answer and is thrown as WWW::PayPal::Error with status, method and path ('PayPal API error (GET /v2/checkout/orders/X): 302 Found'). Commit 3d0cc70.
